 plying for hire were generally accompanied by their formidable mothers.", "reference": "2014 August 15, Nicholas Fogg, Stratford-upon-Avon: The Biography:"}, {"text": "Mop Fairs: Today's annual events are the modern version of the old hiring fairs, where people attended seeking employment or to change it. They are named after the practice of hopefully skilled employees carrying tassels, known as mops, in their buttonholes indicating their occupation. Those who had no trade carried a mop head. At the end of the following week, they could change employers or employees, at what was called the Runaway Mop.", "reference": "2022, Graham Sutherland, Secret Warwick:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(British, obsolete) A tassel worn in a buttonhole to indicate ones occupation in such a fair.", "tags": ["British", "countable", "obsolete", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mop Fairs: Today's annual events are the modern version of the old hiring fairs, where people attended seeking employment or to change it. They are named after the practice of hopefully skilled employees carrying tassels, known as mops, in their buttonholes indicating their occupation. Those who had no trade carried a mop head. At the end of the following week, they could change employers or employees, at what was called the Runaway Mop.", "reference": "2022, Graham Sutherland, Secret Warwick:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(African-American Vernacular, MLE, slang) A firearm particularly if it has a large magazine (compare broom, but still can be related to MP)", "tags": ["Multicultural London English", "countable", "slang", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mainstream in this ting but I'm fully on opps\nGot shot with a mop but that boy never dropped", "reference": "2021 July 4, M24, “Plugged In”, Fumez the Engineer (music), 2:16–2:19:"}], "synonyms": ["fi